Below is a step‐by‐step decomposition of the mission description into a goal model for a multi‐robot system. The mission is to deliver food from the kitchen to an inpatient room, with additional responsibilities for retrieving dirty dishes and opening the room door when needed. Uncertainty exists regarding whether the inpatient (or a companion/nurse) can retrieve food from the tray, and different methods (direct delivery versus tray retrieval) as well as different cooperation modes (robot–robot, robot–human) are available. The following goal model uses hierarchical AND/OR (and fallback) decompositions to capture these alternatives and dependencies.

──────────────────────────────
Step 1. Top-Level Goal

We define a top-level achieve goal that represents the overall service delivery. All sub‐goals must be completed for mission success.

• G1: Complete Hospital Food Service Delivery  
 – Text: “Complete the overall hospital food service delivery including food delivery, dish retrieval, and door opening if required.”  
 – Runtime: “#” (parallel execution) because the three service functions are independent and can be executed concurrently.  
 – Goal Type: Achieve  
 – Target Condition: “Food delivered to inpatient room, dirty dishes retrieved, door opened if necessary.”  
 – Relation: AND  
 – Justification: All service functions (food delivery, dish retrieval, door opening) must be completed for a successful mission.

──────────────────────────────
Step 2. Food Delivery Sub-Goals

Food delivery has two alternatives. Either the robot delivers food directly to the table or it retrieves food from its tray (which requires cooperation). This is modeled as an OR decomposition.

